1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,743
<font color="#FFFFFF">RNA-protein interactions drive gene regulation,</font>

2
00:00:03,823 --> 00:00:07,208
<font color="#FFFFFF">yet measuring them in the lab is slow and costly,</font>

3
00:00:07,288 --> 00:00:11,803
<font color="#FFFFFF">and existing predictors lean on small,
protein-specific datasets.</font>

4
00:00:11,883 --> 00:00:14,137
<font color="#FFFFFF">This work introduces RNAInterAct,</font>

5
00:00:14,217 --> 00:00:19,793
<font color="#FFFFFF">a large curated dataset of non-coding RNA-protein
interactions, and RPIembeddor,</font>

6
00:00:19,873 --> 00:00:26,863
<font color="#FFFFFF">a transformer model that classifies whether any RNA
and protein interact using only their sequences.</font>

7
00:00:26,943 --> 00:00:30,328
<font color="#FFFFFF">By feeding embeddings from two foundation models,</font>

8
00:00:30,408 --> 00:00:35,348
<font color="#FFFFFF">RNA-FM for RNA and ESM-2 for proteins, into an
attention-based network,</font>

9
00:00:35,428 --> 00:00:43,620
<font color="#FFFFFF">RPIembeddor outperforms prior state-of-the-art
methods and generalizes to unseen RNA families and data distributions.</font>

10
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:49,322
<font color="#FFFFFF">Non-coding RNAs regulate the cell largely through
their interactions with proteins,</font>

11
00:00:49,402 --> 00:00:53,552
<font color="#FFFFFF">but mapping these interactions experimentally is
slow and costly.</font>

12
00:00:53,632 --> 00:00:59,605
<font color="#FFFFFF">Most computational predictors sidestep the general
problem by training one model per protein,</font>

13
00:00:59,685 --> 00:01:04,096
<font color="#FFFFFF">which requires a large interaction dataset for that
specific protein.</font>

14
00:01:04,176 --> 00:01:10,474
<font color="#FFFFFF">Such datasets exist for only a few hundred of the
roughly two thousand human RNA-binding proteins.</font>

15
00:01:10,554 --> 00:01:15,485
<font color="#FFFFFF">What is missing is a method that decides, for any
given RNA and protein pair,</font>

16
00:01:15,565 --> 00:01:19,130
<font color="#FFFFFF">whether they interact using nothing but their sequences.</font>

17
00:01:19,510 --> 00:01:23,158
<font color="#FFFFFF">Recent progress shows two useful ideas.</font>

18
00:01:23,238 --> 00:01:26,886
<font color="#FFFFFF">First, learning across many tasks, rather than one
protein at a time,</font>

19
00:01:26,966 --> 00:01:30,613
<font color="#FFFFFF">can help when labeled data is scarce.</font>

20
00:01:30,693 --> 00:01:34,341
<font color="#FFFFFF">Second,</font>

21
00:01:34,421 --> 00:01:38,069
<font color="#FFFFFF">foundation models trained on huge unlabeled
biological corpora capture structural and</font>

22
00:01:38,149 --> 00:01:41,797
<font color="#FFFFFF">functional signal that raw sequences do not expose
directly.</font>

23
00:01:41,877 --> 00:01:45,524
<font color="#FFFFFF">Combining these ideas,</font>

24
00:01:45,604 --> 00:01:49,252
<font color="#FFFFFF">a single model could learn general rules of
RNA-protein binding and apply them to interaction types it has never seen,</font>

25
00:01:49,332 --> 00:01:52,980
<font color="#FFFFFF">which is exactly what a broad, sequence-only
predictor needs.</font>

26
00:01:53,360 --> 00:01:55,569
<font color="#FFFFFF">The work makes three contributions.</font>

27
00:01:55,649 --> 00:01:57,008
<font color="#FFFFFF">It builds RNAInterAct,</font>

28
00:01:57,088 --> 00:02:02,568
<font color="#FFFFFF">an extensive dataset of non-coding RNA-protein
interactions derived from the RNAInter</font>

29
00:02:02,648 --> 00:02:06,297
<font color="#FFFFFF">database and enriched with carefully generated negatives.</font>

30
00:02:06,377 --> 00:02:07,998
<font color="#FFFFFF">It introduces RPIembeddor,</font>

31
00:02:08,078 --> 00:02:13,623
<font color="#FFFFFF">a transformer that classifies interactions from
sequence embeddings and beats existing</font>

32
00:02:13,703 --> 00:02:16,043
<font color="#FFFFFF">tools while generalizing to new data.</font>

33
00:02:16,123 --> 00:02:21,734
<font color="#FFFFFF">And through an ablation study it shows that the two
foundation-model embeddings are not</font>

34
00:02:21,814 --> 00:02:26,640
<font color="#FFFFFF">optional add-ons but the core of the model's
ability to classify correctly.</font>

35
00:02:27,020 --> 00:02:32,910
<font color="#FFFFFF">RPIembeddor turns sequences into knowledge by
leaning on two pre-trained foundation models.</font>

36
00:02:32,990 --> 00:02:35,469
<font color="#FFFFFF">RNA sequences are embedded with RNA-FM,</font>

37
00:02:35,549 --> 00:02:40,848
<font color="#FFFFFF">trained on twenty-three million non-coding RNAs,
and protein sequences with ESM-2,</font>

38
00:02:40,928 --> 00:02:44,784
<font color="#FFFFFF">which predicts folding without multiple sequence
alignments.</font>

39
00:02:44,864 --> 00:02:48,393
<font color="#FFFFFF">Both produce embeddings of size N by six hundred forty.</font>

40
00:02:48,473 --> 00:02:52,001
<font color="#FFFFFF">Two parallel feed-forward layers normalize their sizes,</font>

41
00:02:52,081 --> 00:02:57,774
<font color="#FFFFFF">and encoder layers process the RNA and protein
embeddings symmetrically so attention can</font>

42
00:02:57,854 --> 00:03:02,039
<font color="#FFFFFF">focus on the parts of each sequence most relevant
to interaction.</font>

43
00:03:02,119 --> 00:03:08,009
<font color="#FFFFFF">The latent representations are concatenated and
passed through further feed-forward layers,</font>

44
00:03:08,089 --> 00:03:13,519
<font color="#FFFFFF">ending in a linear layer with a sigmoid that
outputs the probability of interaction.</font>

45
00:03:13,599 --> 00:03:17,390
<font color="#FFFFFF">The whole model has just one-point-four million
parameters.</font>

46
00:03:17,770 --> 00:03:20,625
<font color="#FFFFFF">The dataset is the backbone of the study.</font>

47
00:03:20,705 --> 00:03:25,706
<font color="#FFFFFF">Starting from RNAInter, with over forty-seven
million RNA interactions,</font>

48
00:03:25,786 --> 00:03:31,218
<font color="#FFFFFF">the authors recover sequences by cross-referencing
NCBI, UniProt and Ensembl,</font>

49
00:03:31,298 --> 00:03:35,655
<font color="#FFFFFF">and assign RNA families from Rfam and protein clans
from Pfam.</font>

50
00:03:35,735 --> 00:03:42,669
<font color="#FFFFFF">Those annotations let them generate negatives that
are biologically meaningful rather than random.</font>

51
00:03:42,749 --> 00:03:48,753
<font color="#FFFFFF">The final RNAInterAct set holds about one hundred
twenty-two thousand interactions at</font>

52
00:03:48,833 --> 00:03:51,616
<font color="#FFFFFF">a one-to-two positive-to-negative ratio.</font>

53
00:03:51,696 --> 00:03:54,264
<font color="#FFFFFF">Crucially, it is split by RNA family,</font>

54
00:03:54,344 --> 00:03:57,843
<font color="#FFFFFF">so no family appears in both training and testing.</font>

55
00:03:57,923 --> 00:04:03,211
<font color="#FFFFFF">This homology-aware split, plus evaluation on the
external RPI2825 dataset,</font>

56
00:04:03,291 --> 00:04:06,790
<font color="#FFFFFF">tests true generalization instead of memorization.</font>

57
00:04:07,170 --> 00:04:11,998
<font color="#FFFFFF">On the hardest test, TSfam, where no RNA family
overlaps with training,</font>

58
00:04:12,078 --> 00:04:19,462
<font color="#FFFFFF">RPIembeddor reaches an F1 score of about
zero-point-five-nine and an accuracy of about zero-point-six-seven.</font>

59
00:04:19,542 --> 00:04:23,195
<font color="#FFFFFF">Its ROC area under the curve is zero-point-seven-zero,</font>

60
00:04:23,275 --> 00:04:29,900
<font color="#FFFFFF">while the competing tools XRPI and IPMiner sit at
zero-point-four-eight and zero-point-five-zero,</font>

61
00:04:29,980 --> 00:04:31,213
<font color="#FFFFFF">essentially chance.</font>

62
00:04:31,293 --> 00:04:32,457
<font color="#FFFFFF">In concrete terms,</font>

63
00:04:32,537 --> 00:04:38,540
<font color="#FFFFFF">RPIembeddor correctly labels nearly three thousand
of the positive interactions and over</font>

64
00:04:38,620 --> 00:04:40,682
<font color="#FFFFFF">five thousand of the negatives,</font>

65
00:04:40,762 --> 00:04:44,968
<font color="#FFFFFF">whereas XRPI simply predicts almost everything as
interacting.</font>

66
00:04:45,048 --> 00:04:50,290
<font color="#FFFFFF">The model clearly learns real signal that
generalizes to unseen RNA families.</font>

67
00:04:50,670 --> 00:04:53,731
<font color="#FFFFFF">To test whether the two embeddings really matter,</font>

68
00:04:53,811 --> 00:04:58,026
<font color="#FFFFFF">the authors retrain the model after swapping out
the RNA embedding,</font>

69
00:04:58,106 --> 00:05:02,641
<font color="#FFFFFF">the protein embedding, or both, for random vectors
or one-hot encodings.</font>

70
00:05:02,721 --> 00:05:06,167
<font color="#FFFFFF">In every one of these variants the model stops working,</font>

71
00:05:06,247 --> 00:05:08,410
<font color="#FFFFFF">predicting only the negative class,</font>

72
00:05:08,490 --> 00:05:14,885
<font color="#FFFFFF">so its F1 score falls to zero and its accuracy
merely reflects the fraction of negatives in the data.</font>

73
00:05:14,965 --> 00:05:20,846
<font color="#FFFFFF">Only when both the RNA-FM and ESM-2 embeddings are
present does the model classify correctly.</font>

74
00:05:20,926 --> 00:05:26,359
<font color="#FFFFFF">This confirms that the foundation-model embeddings
carry the structural and functional</font>

75
00:05:26,439 --> 00:05:28,410
<font color="#FFFFFF">information the task depends on.</font>

76
00:05:28,790 --> 00:05:31,032
<font color="#FFFFFF">A few numbers capture the impact.</font>

77
00:05:31,112 --> 00:05:38,489
<font color="#FFFFFF">RPIembeddor scores a ROC area under the curve of
zero-point-seven-zero on the homology-separated test set,</font>

78
00:05:38,569 --> 00:05:42,710
<font color="#FFFFFF">where the best competitor manages only
zero-point-five-zero.</font>

79
00:05:42,790 --> 00:05:48,690
<font color="#FFFFFF">Its F1 of zero-point-five-nine and accuracy of
zero-point-six-seven lead all methods.</font>

80
00:05:48,770 --> 00:05:54,670
<font color="#FFFFFF">The RNAInterAct dataset contributes over one
hundred twenty-two thousand interactions</font>

81
00:05:54,750 --> 00:05:57,977
<font color="#FFFFFF">spanning nine hundred seventy-six RNA families.</font>

82
00:05:58,057 --> 00:06:04,098
<font color="#FFFFFF">And all of this runs in a compact
one-point-four-million-parameter model powered by the</font>

83
00:06:04,178 --> 00:06:06,560
<font color="#FFFFFF">RNA-FM and ESM-2 foundation models.</font>

84
00:06:06,940 --> 00:06:12,965
<font color="#FFFFFF">The lasting message is that general RNA-protein
interaction prediction from sequence alone</font>

85
00:06:13,045 --> 00:06:17,509
<font color="#FFFFFF">is achievable when you stand on the shoulders of
foundation models.</font>

86
00:06:17,589 --> 00:06:24,225
<font color="#FFFFFF">A compact attention network fed with RNA-FM and
ESM-2 embeddings outperforms specialized tools and,</font>

87
00:06:24,305 --> 00:06:28,227
<font color="#FFFFFF">unlike them, generalizes to RNA families it has
never seen.</font>

88
00:06:28,307 --> 00:06:32,635
<font color="#FFFFFF">The companion RNAInterAct dataset, split to remove
homology bias,</font>

89
00:06:32,715 --> 00:06:35,145
<font color="#FFFFFF">gives the community a fair benchmark.</font>

90
00:06:35,225 --> 00:06:37,180
<font color="#FFFFFF">Both embeddings are essential,</font>

91
00:06:37,260 --> 00:06:42,810
<font color="#FFFFFF">and the authors point toward adding RNA-structure
models and longer sequences next.</font>
